                    briefing_title_r1:      `Round 1 — Diagnose disengagement`,
                    briefing_text_r1:       `Engagement fell from <strong>74 to 58</strong> in 18 months, the bottom-quartile teams sit at 44, the insurer account's CSAT dropped to 71% and its team is leaving at <strong>41%</strong>. Move past the headline score: the people-analytics data shows engagement varies more <em>between managers than between sites</em> — a signal that the lever is local leadership, not a company-wide perk. Name the single highest-leverage driver and the segment where intervention will actually move the renewal-relevant metrics. Be honest about whether disengagement <em>caused</em> the attrition and CSAT decline or merely coincided with it.`,
                    briefing_title_r2:      `Round 2 — Choose the intervention portfolio`,
                    briefing_text_r2:       `Allocate the <strong>EUR 1.8M</strong> one-off fund across the menu. The trap is spreading thin "to be fair": eight initiatives at €225k each moved engagement two points — inside the margin of error — and the board froze the recurring fund because nothing was measurable. Concentrate where the causal link to attrition and CSAT is strongest. And decide responsibly: relaunching volunteering builds genuine purpose <em>only when</em> you also fix the at-risk team's pay and workload — otherwise it is a community party while the client's service stays broken.`,
                    briefing_title_r3:      `Round 3 — Design the measurement plan`,
                    briefing_text_r3:       `The board will not release the recurring <strong>EUR 600,000</strong> without proof. Separate <strong>leading</strong> indicators (engagement pulse, volunteering participation, manager-behaviour ratings) that move first from <strong>lagging</strong> business outcomes (attrition, CSAT, renewal). Set a baseline, build a comparison — a phased rollout so untreated sites act as a control — and state a defensible ROI: <em>if attrition falls X points we avoid EUR Y in replacement cost and protect EUR 32M of revenue</em>. Vanity activity metrics (training hours, volunteering days) are explicitly penalized — they measure effort, not outcome.`,
                    briefing_title_r4:      `Round 4 — Defend the plan to the board and client`,
                    briefing_text_r4:       `Three minutes in front of the CFO — who challenges your ROI and the cost of the volunteering relaunch — and then the insurer's procurement lead, who wants evidence of "workforce stability" before renewing. Pick the single headline metric you stake your credibility on, and reconcile the <strong>responsible-leadership story</strong> (purpose, meaningfulness, community) with the <strong>hard commercial case</strong> (retention, CSAT, renewal). Shantz's core argument: engagement and responsibility <em>are</em> business strategy, not philanthropy. A spreadsheet-only pitch loses the purpose the client and your people bought into.`,

                    r1_driver_label:        `Name the single highest-leverage driver of disengagement.`,
                    r1_segment_label:       `Where do you target the intervention to move the renewal-relevant metrics?`,
                    r1_causal_label:        `How confident are you that disengagement CAUSED the attrition and CSAT decline (not just coincided)? (%)`,
                    r1_causal_readout:      `Confidence in the causal link to renewal-relevant metrics:`,
                    driver_managers:        `Manager quality — engagement varies more between managers than between sites; frontline leadership is the lever`,
