                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_managers:      `<strong>That's the data-supported diagnosis.</strong> Engagement varies more between managers than between sites — so the bottom-quartile manager cohort is your cheapest, highest-leverage lever, not a company-wide perk. Fix the leaders and the team's engagement, attrition and CSAT follow.`,
                    coach_r1_pay:           `<strong>Pay and workload matter on the at-risk team — but they're not the whole story.</strong> The variance is between managers, not just between pay grades. Underpay is real on the insurer account; just don't mistake it for the company-wide driver.`,
                    coach_r1_purpose:       `<strong>Lost purpose is a real symptom, but watch the causality.</strong> "The community stuff disappeared" shows up in exit interviews, yet the engagement variance tracks managers. Volunteering rebuilds purpose — it won't fix a team that's underpaid, overloaded and badly led.`,
                    coach_r1_score:         `<strong>"It fell 16 points company-wide, treat it as one problem" is exactly the headline-score trap.</strong> The score is an average hiding a bimodal reality — bottom-quartile teams at 44, others fine. Diagnose the segment, or you'll spend the fund where the problem isn't.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> Manager quality as the driver AND the at-risk team plus bottom-quartile managers as the segment — that targets exactly where attrition and CSAT bite and where the renewal is decided. Now concentrate the fund to match.`,
                    coach_r1_allstaff:      `<strong>"All 2,100 equally, so no one feels singled out" spreads the signal to zero.</strong> A company-wide perk dilutes the spend away from the insurer team and the weak managers — the only place the renewal-relevant metrics actually move.`,
                    coach_r1_topteams:      `<strong>Rewarding your best teams feels nice and changes nothing the client cares about.</strong> The at-risk account is leaving at 41% — that's where the EUR 32M renewal is won or lost, not in the teams already engaged.`,
                    coach_r1_overconfident: `<strong>Easy on the certainty.</strong> Claiming you're almost certain engagement caused the decline is the over-claim the CFO will puncture — "compared to what?" Hold a confident but defensible link, and prove it with a baseline and a control later.`,
                    coach_r1_undersure:     `<strong>If you barely believe disengagement caused this, why invest €1.8M against it?</strong> The exit interviews, the manager variance and the CSAT/attrition correlation are real evidence. Make the causal case with conviction — then measure it to confirm.`,
                    coach_r2_concentrated:  `<strong>That's concentration, not dilution.</strong> Weighting manager-capability and the at-risk team's pay/workload puts the money where the causal link to attrition and CSAT is strongest — and that's a signal the board can actually measure.`,
                    coach_r2_spreadthin:    `<strong>You funded everything at roughly €0.36M "to be fair".</strong> In six months engagement moved a couple of points — inside the survey's margin of error — so the board froze the recurring €0.6M because nothing was measurable. The data told you manager quality drove the variance. Why fund equally instead of where the signal was?`,
                    coach_r2_volunteertrap: `<strong>Great volunteering relaunch — participation will jump.</strong> But you left the insurer team underpaid and overloaded, so they keep leaving at 41% and CSAT doesn't move. The client noticed you threw a community party while their service stayed broken. Was volunteering the cure, or a distraction from the real problem?`,
                    coach_r2_mixed:         `<strong>A workable mix — but tilt it harder.</strong> The more weight on the manager-capability programme and the at-risk team's pay/workload, and the less spread evenly across feel-good lines, the clearer the signal and the stronger the renewal case.`,
                    coach_r3_pulse:         `<strong>That's the right leading indicator.</strong> The engagement pulse, volunteering participation and manager-behaviour ratings move before attrition and CSAT do — they give you the early honest signal that the plan is working, months before the lagging business outcomes confirm it.`,
                    coach_r3_csatnow:       `<strong>Watching only the lagging numbers leaves you blind for months.</strong> Attrition and CSAT move last. Without a leading indicator you won't know the plan is working until the renewal window has nearly closed — too late to course-correct.`,
                    coach_r3_vanity:        `<strong>Your dashboard shows training hours and volunteering days. The CFO asks one question: did attrition fall and will we keep the contract?</strong> You can't answer — you measured effort, not outcome. Which of those numbers would actually convince procurement?`,
                    coach_r3_nobaseline:    `<strong>You assert the programme "improved engagement". Compared to what?</strong> Rolled out everywhere at once with no baseline and no untreated site, you can't separate your effect from a labour market that just loosened. A phased rollout gives you the comparison group that proves it was you.`,
                    coach_r3_sharp:         `<strong>That's a measurement plan that survives the CFO.</strong> A leading pulse, a phased rollout as your control group, and an avoided-attrition ROI tied to the €9,500 replacement cost and the €32M contract — leading and lagging cleanly separated, causality defensible, the return in euros.`,
                    coach_r3_horizonshort:  `<strong>One or two months is naive — the lagging metrics lag.</strong> Promise the board attrition will fall by next month and you'll be caught short; engagement moves first, then attrition over a couple of quarters. Set an honest horizon or you'll lose credibility when the numbers don't move on cue.`,
                    coach_r4_retention:     `<strong>Attrition to the benchmark is the metric the client asked for.</strong> It directly answers the "workforce stability" condition and converts straight into avoided replacement cost and protected revenue — the number that wins the renewal and the CFO in the same sentence.`,
                    coach_r4_engagement:    `<strong>Engagement is your leading indicator, not your headline.</strong> Staking the case on the score back toward 74 is honest but it's a proxy — the client renews on stability and CSAT. Lead with the lagging outcome and use engagement as the early evidence behind it.`,
                    coach_r4_hours:         `<strong>"Look how hard we worked" is the activity-metric trap, again, at the worst possible moment.</strong> The CFO and procurement don't buy hours delivered. Stake your credibility on attrition and renewal, or you'll walk out of that room with neither the money nor the contract.`,
                    coach_r4_integrated:    `<strong>That's Shantz's argument made operational.</strong> Engagement and responsibility ARE the business strategy — you reconciled the retention savings with the purpose the client and your people bought into. The CFO gets the euros; the procurement lead and your staff get the meaning. Both, not either.`,
                    coach_r4_costonly:      `<strong>You won the money on a pure cost-cutting case — and lost the plot.</strong> Your CSR lead is right: employees and the client bought into Liffey Connect's purpose, and a spreadsheet-only narrative won't rebuild that. Where did meaningfulness go?`,
                    coach_r4_purposeonly:   `<strong>A pure purpose pitch melts under the CFO's first question.</strong> "The numbers will follow" is not an ROI. Lead with community and meaning alone and you hand the sceptic the easy win — pair the purpose with the retention euros.`,
                    coach_r4_cut:           `<strong>Cutting volunteering entirely overcorrects.</strong> The trap was leaning on it INSTEAD of fixing fundamentals — not running it at all. Well-designed, measured volunteering complements the pay and manager fixes and rebuilds the purpose the award was built on. Keep it, and measure it.`,
                    coach_r4_facedown:      `<strong>That faces them both down.</strong> A retention headline the client can verify, an integrated purpose-and-performance story, and a volunteering line defended as a measured intervention with a tracked target — that's a pitch that wins the recurring fund and the EUR 32M renewal together.`,

                    sum_default:            `Decision made.`,
