                    insight_renewed:        `Contract renewed & engagement restored: you diagnosed the manager-quality driver, concentrated the fund on the at-risk team, measured it with a leading pulse and a phased control, and pitched an integrated case. Attrition fell to benchmark, CSAT recovered, the board released the recurring €0.6M and the insurer renewed EUR 32M.`,
                    insight_spread:         `No measurable signal: you funded the menu near-evenly "to be fair", so engagement moved inside the margin of error and the board froze the recurring €0.6M. The data told you manager quality drove the variance — concentration, not dilution, was the lesson.`,
                    insight_volunteer:      `Community party, service still broken: the volunteering relaunch lifted participation, but with the at-risk team still underpaid and overloaded the attrition and CSAT the client cares about didn't move. Volunteering complements fixing fundamentals; it cannot substitute for it.`,
                    insight_short:          `You didn't fully connect diagnosis, spend, measurement and pitch. The plan moved some metrics but the renewal case still has a seam the CFO or procurement could pull on.`,
                    insight_vanity:         `Your dashboard leaned on activity metrics — training hours, volunteering days. The CFO's question "did attrition fall and will we keep the contract?" needs outcome metrics, not effort. That's the leading/lagging error the board was watching for.`,
                    insight_nobaseline:     `You claimed ROI without a baseline or a comparison group. Rolled out everywhere at once, you can't prove it was your plan and not a loosening labour market — a phased rollout would have given you the control the CFO demanded.`,
                    insight_costonly:       `You stripped the responsible-leadership story to a pure cost-cutting case. You may have won the money, but the purpose the client and your people bought into went missing — and that's the engagement that differentiated the firm.`,
                    insight_retained:       `At-risk-team attrition came down to (or below) the ~25% benchmark — the workforce-stability condition the client set is met, and it converts directly into avoided replacement cost and protected revenue.`,
                    insight_roi:            `Return on people, in euros: every point you took off the at-risk team's attrition is ~310 roles × the €9,500 replacement cost avoided — the people-case made in the same language as any capital decision.`,

                    hint_spread:            `You've spread the fund thin — engagement will move inside the margin of error and the recurring tranche stays frozen. Concentrate on the manager-capability programme and the at-risk team's pay/workload.`,
                    hint_volunteer:         `Volunteering is doing the heavy lifting while pay and workload on the at-risk team go unfixed. Re-balance toward the fundamentals — volunteering complements them, it can't replace them.`,
                    hint_vanity:            `Your plan is leaning on activity metrics. Swap hours and days delivered for outcome metrics — attrition, CSAT, renewal — or the CFO won't release the recurring fund.`,
                    hint_nobaseline:        `Your ROI has no baseline or control. Add a phased rollout so untreated sites prove it was your plan, not the labour market.`,
                    hint_attrition:         `At-risk-team attrition is still high — the very number the client is watching. Weight the fund toward the pay/workload fix and the manager-capability programme on that team.`,
                    hint_strong:            `Strong position — engagement up, attrition down, renewal confidence healthy. Lock in the retention headline and the integrated purpose-and-performance pitch to hold it.`,

